# Migrations

Writing a migration is the agent's job; applying it to a real database is the user's or CI's
(`core.mdc` → Deleting data). The guard holds that line, not memory (below).

## Atomic: the old schema or the new one, never between

A deploy can die between any two statements: cancelled, connection dropped, server crash. So each
migration file is one transaction and lands whole or not at all. Proof:
`stacks/sql-migrations/kill-partway.test.mjs` strikes after every statement of a fixture and finds
the old or new schema each time; without BEGIN/COMMIT it finds one between.

| Tool | Wraps each file? | `.squawk.toml` `assume_in_transaction` | You write |
|---|---|---|---|
| Prisma, plain `psql -f` | no: statements run one by one | `false` | `BEGIN;` first, `COMMIT;` last, in any file with more than one statement |
| goose | yes, unless `-- +goose NO TRANSACTION` | `true` | no BEGIN/COMMIT |
| sqlx | yes, unless the first line is `-- no-transaction` | `true` | no BEGIN/COMMIT |
| dbmate | yes, unless `transaction:false` | `true` | no BEGIN/COMMIT |
| Alembic | yes on Postgres (transactional DDL) | n/a: revisions are Python, not linted | no `COMMIT` in a revision |

- `pnpm migrations:lint` refuses a multi-statement file outside a transaction when the tool doesn't
  wrap, and squawk refuses a BEGIN inside one that does.
- **Prisma:** generate with `prisma migrate dev --create-only`, then add `BEGIN;` / `COMMIT;` around
  the generated SQL. Measured on Prisma 7.10 and Postgres 16: a failing statement then leaves the
  old schema; without them, the statements before it stay applied. Prisma reports such a failure
  as "current transaction is aborted"; the real error shows with
  `psql -v ON_ERROR_STOP=1 -f migration.sql` on a loopback copy. Either way `_prisma_migrations`
  keeps a failed row and the next deploy refuses: `prisma migrate resolve --rolled-back <name>`,
  fix, deploy again.
- **What can't run in a transaction** (`CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY`, `VACUUM`, `ALTER SYSTEM`) goes
  alone in its own file, with only `SET` beside it and the tool's no-transaction marker where it has
  one (dbmate: `-- migrate:up transaction:false`). Prisma: one statement
  per file, or its parser may send the file as one implicit transaction and every CONCURRENTLY fails.
  Killed partway, a concurrent build leaves an INVALID index that `IF NOT EXISTS` then skips; the
  file's header says the recovery: `DROP INDEX CONCURRENTLY IF EXISTS <name>`, mark the migration
  rolled back, deploy again.

## Live tables (squawk)

`pnpm migrations:lint` runs squawk on every `*.sql` / `*.pgsql` (any case) under a `migrations/` or
`migration/` directory (any case; the list is a knob in `lint.mjs`), not `*.down.sql`: in `pnpm
check`, CI's Stack pack checks, and the edit hook on each migration. squawk reads only the Up
section of a goose or dbmate file. The full run names any `*.sql` it found outside those
directories. A finding names its rule; the fix is at `https://squawkhq.com/docs/<rule>`.

- **Index on an existing table:** `CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY IF NOT EXISTS`, alone in its own
  migration. A plain index blocks every write for the whole build. A table created in the same
  file may take a plain index.
- **`SET LOCAL lock_timeout = '5s';`** at the top of each transaction, so a migration stuck behind a
  lock fails instead of queueing every query behind it. No statement_timeout (excluded in
  `.squawk.toml`: it kills a long build or backfill partway).
- **New constraints `NOT VALID`**, then `VALIDATE CONSTRAINT` in a later migration of its own:
  adding checks nothing and validating takes a weaker lock.
- **`NOT NULL` on an existing table:** add it nullable, backfill, add `CHECK (col IS NOT NULL) NOT
  VALID`, validate; `SET NOT NULL` after that skips the scan.
- **Dropping a column or table:** stop using it in one deploy, drop it in a later one. A drop in a
  migration is a delete (`core.mdc`): the PR says so, and the plan carries a Human check.
- **Deleting rows** (`DELETE`, `TRUNCATE`, `UPDATE … SET col = NULL` or `SET (a, b) = (NULL, …)`,
  in a CTE, a `DO` block, or `MERGE … THEN DELETE` too) needs a `-- data-loss: <human-check-id>`
  comment in the Up section naming that Human check by its kebab-case id (not a placeholder such
  as `TODO` or `HC-?`); the lint refuses the file without one.
- **A rule that doesn't fit one statement:** `-- squawk-ignore <rule>` on it, with the why on the line
  above. Never exclude a rule project-wide to get one file through.
- **Adopting the pack with history:** list migrations already applied in production in
  `.squawk.toml` → `excluded_paths`; they are never edited, so never re-linted. The full run prints
  how many files each entry skips, and refuses an entry that skips a file `origin/dev` doesn't have,
  or one this branch changed since `origin/dev`.

## Invariants and existing rows

- An invariant the app relies on is a `CHECK`, foreign key, or `UNIQUE` in the schema, not only
  app code.
- Every new constraint or `NOT NULL` says how existing rows get there: the backfill in the same
  migration or an earlier one, and what happens to rows that don't fit. Fixture `0003_note_slug.sql`
  is the failure to picture: a backfill that collides on rows nobody looked at.
- QA asks two things of every migration PR: how existing rows are backfilled, and whether a deploy
  signs anyone out (a session or token column changed).

## Writing and changing

- Generated SQL is exempt from the file-header rule (`docs.mdc`). A hand-written comment at the
  top is wanted when the migration does something odd (CONCURRENTLY, a recovery, a backfill rule).
- **Unmerged: fix it in place.** Never stack a second migration to patch one that hasn't merged.
  On rebase, re-timestamp yours so it sorts after the ones merged meanwhile.
- **Merged: it's history.** A database that applied it never re-runs it, so an edit silently
  diverges. Fix forward with a new migration.

## Applying

- **Local:** `migrate dev` / `migrate reset` against localhost only.
- **Real environments:** deploy-only (`prisma migrate deploy`, `goose up`, `sqlx migrate run`), run by
  CI or the user. Deploy order: freeze writes, run the checks, migrate, then roll out.
- A migration that changes existing tables runs against a prod-shaped staging database before it
  ships: a Human check in the plan.
- Never tunnel a real database to a localhost port: the guard, and you, would read it as loopback.
- A `/cloudsql/…` socket is the Cloud SQL proxy to a real database, not loopback. The only socket
  directories that count as this machine are `/tmp`, `/private/tmp`, `/var/run/postgresql`,
  `/run/postgresql`, `/var/run/mysqld`, and `/run/mysqld` (postgres `?host=/dir`; MySQL
  `?socket=/dir/mysqld.sock` by its directory).

## The guard (`scripts/command-guard.mjs`)

It reads where a command points: a URL on the line, `DATABASE_URL` (goose: `GOOSE_DBSTRING`) set
on the line, then the environment, then `.env` where it runs (as dotenv reads it: the last one
wins, `KEY: value`, quoted values over several lines; Prisma's `prisma/.env` too, and a URL written
into `schema.prisma` or `prisma.config.ts`, or the variable its `url` and `directUrl` name, the
worse of the two; `//` comments skipped). Prisma's schema is read where Prisma looks for it: the
defaults, `--schema`, `package.json` → `"prisma": { "schema": … }`, a config's `schema:`, and a
multi-file folder (`prisma/schema/*.prisma`, up to 64 files). `unset X`, `env -u X`, `env -i`, and `sudo` (unless `-E`
or `--preserve-env`) leave only the `.env`. It finds the tool behind a path or a wrapper
(`./node_modules/.bin/prisma`, `node node_modules/prisma/…`, `npx prisma@6`, `timeout`, `sudo`,
`cross-env`, `bash -lc`, `env -S`), follows `cd` (`cd -`, `-P`, bare `cd`, `{ … }`), `pushd`, and
`( … )`, and reads `pnpm <script>`, `npm run <script>`, and `pnpm -C <dir>` through to the command.
Client flags are read the way getopt reads them (`-tAc "…"`, `-c"…"`, `-qf file`, mysql `-ppass`).

- **Refused against a non-loopback database:** `prisma migrate reset`, `db push --accept-data-loss`
  or `--force-reset`, `migrate dev`; sqlx (`db`/`mig` too) and diesel `database drop/reset`,
  `migrate revert`; goose `down/down-to/redo/reset`; golang-migrate `down/drop/force/goto`; dbmate
  `drop/rollback`; `alembic downgrade`; `knex migrate:rollback`; `drizzle-kit push --force`;
  `supabase db reset --linked`; `pg_restore --clean`; a SQL client whose own SQL (`-c`, `-f`, `<`,
  a heredoc, a here-string, `\i`/`\ir`, MySQL `source`, or `echo`/`cat`/`cat <<EOF`/`pg_dump --clean`
  piped into it) drops, truncates, deletes, merges a delete, nulls a column or clears it to `DEFAULT`
  or `''`, or retypes it `USING NULL`, read both as written and with comments removed. A SQL client behind `ssh`, `kubectl exec`, `docker exec`, `su -c`, or
  another wrapper is judged the same way, and there `localhost` is that machine's database.
- **The floor:** under that reading sits dev's older whole-line rule, kept so the reading can only
  tighten it: a line that runs a SQL client and says `DROP TABLE/COLUMN/DATABASE/SCHEMA`, `TRUNCATE`,
  or `DELETE FROM` anywhere, with no `localhost`, `127.x.x.x`, or `::1` on it, is refused. A socket
  directory or a bare `psql` to the default socket is caught by it too: name `-h localhost`.
  Inside a local container (`docker compose exec db psql …`) it asks instead: that is most likely
  the container's own dev database, but the guard can't read it.
- **Asks** when it can't read the target (`$VAR`, a secrets loader, `source`, `eval`, `--schema`
  elsewhere, a Prisma config file, a `cd` it can't follow such as `$DIR`, CDPATH, a missing
  directory, one behind `if`, inside a function or a `case` arm, or after `HOME=` is set, a wrapper
  it doesn't model such as `pnpm --filter` or `docker exec`): before a destructive command always,
  and before any migrate command once the target was hidden. Destructive SQL against a host it
  can't place (a service file, `$HOST`) asks; against a known remote host it is refused. Also asks
  before a deploy to a real database, and before SQL it can't read against one (piped from
  anything else, stdin it can't see, `-c "$SQL"`, `-v` variables, a file that is missing or written
  earlier on the line, more than 20 files or 1 MB of SQL across the whole line,
  `DO`/`EXECUTE`/`\gexec`).
- **Allowed** on loopback, a local socket directory, or a SQLite file; `migrate dev` and `migrate
  deploy` with no target set at all, and `migrate status`, run. `PGHOSTADDR` or `PGSERVICE` in play
  means the host isn't the one on the line. A Prisma reset (`migrate reset`, `db push
  --force-reset`/`--accept-data-loss`) needs positive evidence: it runs only once the guard has read
  a datasource and every `url`/`directUrl` in it is loopback; with no schema it can read, it asks.
- A tool that reads another variable is invisible to it: add a deny to `command-guard.project.json`.
- **A static check reads the line, not the network.** A port-forward or proxy started outside the
  line (`cloud-sql-proxy`, `kubectl port-forward`, `ssh -L`, `fly proxy`) makes `localhost` a real
  database, and the guard still reads it as loopback. Before a reset, know what the port is.
- **The hook reads its own process environment**, which can differ from the agent shell's (a
  variable exported in an earlier command, `direnv`, a shell profile): `DATABASE_URL` as the hook
  saw it may not be what the command gets.

